.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Sabatier and Senderens showed from 1897 that finely divided nickel hydrogenates ethylene (C_2H_4 + H_2 \to C_2H_6) at temperatures where the gas-phase reaction does not run at all. This example uses illustrative Arrhenius parameters and compare_catalyzed_rate to compute the conversion reached in a one-second contact time as the temperature rises. Over nickel the reaction lights off hundreds of kelvin earlier. A second panel shows Sabatier&#x27;s rule of thumb from screening many metals: a metal that binds the reactant too weakly holds almost none of it on the surface, and one that binds it too strongly leaves no free sites. Only a moderate binder has both.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/catalysis/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_sabatier_hydrogenation_thumb.png
